  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Tuesday) \nIssued at 1249 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nA potent upper-level trough will progress east into the Great Plains \nthrough Thursday with a potent cold front moving into the \nPanhandles. A steep pressure gradient will exist behind the front \nwith a 40-45 kt LLJ. This will create windy conditions across the \nPanhandles on Thursday as cooler air surges in. A Wind Advisory may \neventually be needed for at least a portion of the northwestern \ncombined Panhandles.\n\nA much cooler-than-average day is expected on Friday with highs in \nthe 50s, though there are some ensemble members that suggest highs \ncould be in the 40s for portions of the Oklahoma Panhandle and \nnorthern Texas Panhandle. At this time, that is a very low \nprobability (around 10%). Winds will be breezy throughout the day \nbut will begin to weaken in the afternoon as the pressure gradient \nweakens. Frost and freeze products may be needed for Friday night as \nlows are currently forecast to drop into the upper-20s to mid-30s. \n\nThe upper-level trough will track into the Great Lakes/northeastern \nUS Saturday morning while an upper-level ridge builds over the \nWestern US with a cut-off trough over the Southwestern US. \nTemperatures will warm through the weekend as the trough potentially \nbegins to move eastward toward the Panhandles. Depending on the \nquality of moisture return, we could see showers and thunderstorms \nreturn into the region.\n\nVanden Bosch",
